title ALES115 — Lotus's replace, the general span-edit (cut and splice, composed)
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first; after ALES114 sent, take the next agent-doable Lotus lap, self-approve, prove GREEN, send as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think ALES2 gave two exact inverses over a span — cut REMOVES it (the clip shrinks) and splice INSERTS (it grows) — and ALES114's paste_over writes new samples ON TOP the same length. The one gesture that ties all three is paste-into-a-selection: circle a span, drop different audio in its place, and the take changes length by however much longer or shorter the new audio is. Chosen crux-first because replace is the single move that GENERALIZES three proven rungs at once — an empty src is pure cut, a zero count is pure splice, a src.len == count is paste_over — so naming it makes the whole edit family one law with three faces rather than four separate tools.
think Shape: replace(clip, at, count, src) removes [at, at+count) and inserts src at at. It is exactly cut(at, count) then splice(at, src) composed over ALES2's OWN public API — no third primitive, no new error. The discipline is checking ALL bounds BEFORE any write: BadRange when the removed span falls outside the samples (at > len, or count > len - at, without underflow), and ClipFull when the result would run past the fixed buffer (ins_len against the headroom max_clip - len + count, checked without underflow so the refusal lands before the cut mutates a sample). Only once every check passes do the two pre-validated sub-edits run, so no refusal ever lands mid-edit. The payload is a caller slice bounded once against max_clip before @intCast to u32 at the std seam.
think Four laws: the REPLACE law (the span becomes src, the head untouched, the tail after the removed span following the new audio, the length len - count + src.len, proven shorter/longer/equal); the GENERALIZATION law — the crux: empty src equals ALES2's cut byte for byte, zero count equals ALES2's splice, src.len == count equals ALES114's paste_over, each proven against its own rung over one anchor; the LENGTH law (the length changes by exactly src.len - count in all three directions); the DEGENERATE / REFUSAL law (replace(at, 0, empty) the identity, a span past the samples BadRange, a result past the buffer ClipFull, each before any write).
obs One build red, fixed at once: headroom read clip.buf.len (a comptime usize array length) which cannot narrow to u32 — swapped to timeline.max_clip (the u32 constant buf.len equals), and the witness went GREEN. All four laws pass on metal, including the ClipFull headroom refusal proven by filling the clip to max_clip and confirming the pre-cut wrote no sample (buf[0] unchanged, len held). The generalization law is the teaching crux — proving replace's three corners ARE cut, splice, and paste_over makes the edit family read as one parametrized gesture. >=2 positive-invariant asserts per fn; timeline.EditError reused whole; the one seam cast bounds the payload against max_clip first; width-check clean; TAME style bans clean, ratchet zero-assert count unchanged (replace.rye carries its asserts). No custody gate touched — pure-Rye local i16 buffer, siloed to lotus/.
